### Merkle Tree Certificates Update
- **Presenter**: David Benjamin
- **Slides**: [Merkle Tree Certificates Update](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-plants-merkle-tree-certificates-update-00)
- **Draft**: `draft-ietf-plants-merkle-tree-certs`

- **Technical Updates**:
  - Official OIDs have been assigned (replacing Cloudflare's experimental arc), establishing a stable target for 2027 post-quantum deployments.
  - Wire format adjustments include changing length prefixes to 24-bit to accommodate multiple SLH-DSA co-signatures, updating name formats, and embedding hash algorithm encodings implicitly into extension types.
  - Initial IANA considerations were added for three registries using "Specification Required" with private use ranges. David Benjamin will add expert reviewer guidance to verify stable public specifications.
  - Test vectors have been added to the draft, and log serving API specifications were updated in C2SP.
  - Implementation progress includes updates in Chrome (targeting 2027 MTC application acceptance), pending OpenSSL feature branch PRs, proof-of-concept web server patches by Bob Beck, and draft root program policy updates (Chrome policy RC 0.04 and upcoming Apple policies).

- **Discussion**:
  - **ACME Integration**: Mike Ounsworth raised questions regarding ACME certificate provisioning (Section 9). Amir Ghani noted that fetching standalone MTCs via ACME works out-of-the-box for clients supporting CSRs and profiles, while landmark-relative certificates will require additional client scheduling work.
  - **WGLC Readiness**: Dennis Jackson recommended taking time to bake the draft or utilizing Working Group Last Call (WGLC) to catch edge cases without needing a future bis draft. Amir Ghani offered text updates to clarify subtree hash operations relative to RFC 9162.

